Day 39 – Saga Pattern (Distributed Transaction)

The Saga Pattern is a way to manage distributed transactions across multiple services without using a
traditional ACID transaction (which doesn’t scale well in microservices).

🔹 ❌ Problem

In microservices:

Each service has its own database

You can’t use a single DB transaction across services

👉 Example:

Order Service ✅

Payment Service ✅

Inventory Service ❌

Now system is inconsistent (payment taken, but no inventory)

🔹 ✅ Solution: Saga Pattern

A Saga is a sequence of local transactions, where:

Each step updates its own service DB

If something fails → compensating transactions undo previous steps

🔹 🔄 Key Idea

Instead of “rollback”, we use compensation

🔹 Example (E-commerce Flow)
Steps:

Order Created

Payment Processed

Inventory Reserved

Order Confirmed

❌ Failure Scenario

If Inventory fails:

Cancel Payment 💸

Cancel Order ❌

🔹 🔧 Two Types of Saga
1️⃣ Choreography-Based Saga (Event-driven)

No central controller. Services react to events.

🔄 Flow using Apache Kafka:

Order Service → emits OrderCreated

Payment Service → listens → emits PaymentCompleted

Inventory Service → listens → emits success/failure

✅ Pros

Simple

Fully decoupled

Fits event-driven systems

❌ Cons

Hard to debug

Logic is scattered

Difficult to track flow

2️⃣ Orchestration-Based Saga

A central orchestrator controls the flow.

🔄 Flow:

Orchestrator calls:

Payment Service

Inventory Service

Order Service

Example tools:

Temporal

Camunda

✅ Pros

Easy to manage

Clear flow

Better monitoring

❌ Cons

Central dependency

Slightly less flexible

🔹 🔁 Compensation Transactions

Each step must have a reverse action:

Step	Compensation
Payment success	Refund payment
Inventory reserved	Release stock
Order created	Cancel order

👉 These must be:

Idempotent

Reliable

🔹 🔥 Real Fintech Example
Money Transfer:

Debit Account A

Credit Account B

❌ If step 2 fails:

Compensate → credit back Account A

🔹 ⚠️ Challenges

Designing correct compensations

Handling partial failures

Event ordering issues

Debugging distributed flows

🔹 🧠 Best Practices

Use idempotency keys

Combine with Outbox Pattern

Add retry + DLQ

Maintain audit logs

Use timeouts & monitoring

🔹 🆚 Saga vs 2PC (Two-Phase Commit)
Feature	Saga	2PC
Scalability	High	Low
Coupling	Loose	Tight
Performance	Better	Slower
Rollback	Compensation	Atomic rollback

👉 Modern systems prefer Saga over 2PC

🔹 🧩 How it fits with previous topics

Outbox Pattern → reliable event publishing

Kafka → event communication

Retry + DLQ → failure handling

Idempotency → avoid duplicates

🔹 🚀 Final Summary

Saga = sequence of local transactions + compensations

Ensures eventual consistency

Two types:

Choreography (event-driven)

Orchestration (central control)

Critical for microservices & fintech systems